installing the distributed 1.67 on a simulated hard disk
========================================================

	./run

which boots the 1.67 standalone off the simulated DJ/DMA with the
console in this window and nothing else going on.  Nothing is traced
unless you ask for it:

	./run -t 0x100000	system calls and what they returned
	./run -t 0x120000	and every entry to the kernel as well
	./run -c 0x114		console in an xterm of its own instead

Nothing here is built from this tree.  This is the authentic install:
the distributed standalone, on a simulated drive, kept because it is
what the hardware did.  A drive made from the tree does not need a
floppy at all - create_vol builds a bootable volume and boot_vol boots
it, and docs/INSTALLATION.md section 3 is where that path is written down.

Tracing goes to src/hwsim/d1/logfile - run adds -l when it sees -t,
because trace output goes to a file descriptor and -l is what points that
descriptor at the log, and because otherwise it would land on top of the
console.  Watch it from another window with tail -f.

Each line names the task and the address, so

	tsk1:43f3 micronix stat("/dev/ttyA", f7db

can be handed straight back as -T tsk1:43f3 to start an instruction trace
there, or -W <addr> to watch writes to a byte.

Wait for the welcome, and then for the shell's prompt, which is

	#

If the welcome appears and no prompt follows, the machine is wedged - it
is not a shell waiting quietly.  That distinction cost most of a day
here, because a wedged machine and a shell with nothing to say look
identical if you believe there is no prompt.  What the wedge looks like
from outside: system calls stop - the last one is init's
stat("/dev/ttyA") - while clock interrupts keep arriving, tens of
thousands of them, every one of them landing at tsk1:003d.  The processor
is halted there waiting for something to run and the scheduler never
finds it.

	tail -f ../../src/hwsim/d1/logfile

and watch whether the syscall count moves.  If it is frozen and the
interrupt count is climbing, that is this.

The last line of the welcome is

	Then boot Micronix from the hard disk. If you have any
	questions, please read the Installation section of the
	Micronix User's Manual.

Anything typed before the prompt is thrown away when the shell sets up
the terminal.  When the # appears, type

	source m5init

and it will mkfs /dev/m5a, fsck it, mount it on /b, copy the running
system across with cptree, relink /b/dev/root and /b/dev/swap, patch the
kernel's root and swap devices with ddt, tidy up and unmount.  It prints
"Disk initialization complete." when it is done.  cptree is copying a
whole filesystem through a simulated Z80, so give it time.

about swap, and a wrong turn worth not repeating
------------------------------------------------

The disk ships with

	root dev: djdma/76
	swap dev: nodev/0

and that is correct - it is not a missing setting.  fork only swaps a
child out if there is a swap device:

	bankcopy(child);
	if (swapdev && swapout(child)) run(swapproc);

so with nodev the standalone runs entirely in core, which is what a
floppy-only system wants.  Pointing swap at a drive turns a system that
does not swap into one that does, and then it spends its time in the
scheduler walking twenty process slots looking for something to bring
back.  Do not "fix" it.

"no more space"
---------------

"No more space on disk djdma/76" is the floppy filling up, and the usual
reason is that the mount failed: m5init runs "mount /dev/m5a /b" and then
"cptree / /b", and if the mount did not take then cptree copies the whole
system into the directory /b on the floppy instead of onto the hard disk,
which fills 760 blocks quickly.  The system call trace in the runner
window shows whether the mount worked, and mkfs before it.

Either way, start again from a clean disk - run discards the deltas, so
just run it again rather than trying to tidy up inside a filesystem that
has already been half filled.

what is here
------------

    standalone167.bin	the 1.67 standalone, exactly as distributed
    sa67.5.skew.1k	the name that gives it its geometry, via bdev(2,93)
    hddma-0.blessed	a drive that boots, carrying the original userland
    run			boots the standalone above and drives this install

A drive a run writes into is named with a .vol suffix, and no .vol is
tracked - what is here ending in .vol is what the last run or experiment
left behind.  A drive worth keeping is kept the way hddma-0.blessed is,
under a name that says what it is.  Nothing else here carries the
suffix, which is what lets .gitignore tell the images from the scripts.

The other scripts are the host-side path and REGEN.md is where they are:
create_vol builds a bootable volume out of the tree, boot_vol boots one,
check_vol runs the host checkers over one, and ncr_create_vol and
ncr_boot_vol do the same pair for the NCR 5380.

Nothing is patched.  An earlier version of this directory had the swap
device changed and that was a mistake - see above.

afterwards
----------

m5init does not write a boot block, and nothing else does either: the rom
reads cylinder 0 head 0 sector 0 of the hard disk and there is nothing
there.  See hwsim/DISKS for what that takes - micronix/stand builds the
two levels and mkfs -i puts them in a file that owns cylinder 0, which is
what keeps the filesystem from allocating over them.

Use 1.67 and not 1.6.  1.6's kernel has its root device as djdma/12,
which is the single sided minor for a disk that is double sided, and it
stops in the rom monitor before reaching a shell.  1.67 has 76.

the full install on two drives, and the native compile
------------------------------------------------------

A different path from ./run: build two blank volumes from the tree, install
all nine distribution diskettes onto them, and compile the source tree the
install just put on /usr/src.  build_2drive makes the volumes and stamps
install5.login as /.login, so a boot needs nothing typed.

    ./build_2drive                 # /tmp/q540root.img + /tmp/q540usr.img

Each boot is the d1 command below, with the drives and the floppies it
names.  The root and usr volumes are the two hard disks; the four djdma
units are the distribution diskettes of that pass.  Watch /tmp/boot.log
for "=== PASS-DONE" (install) or "=== COMPILE-DONE" (compile).

pass 1 - disks 1-4 (install5.login is already /.login):

    cd ../../src/hwsim/d1
    ./d1 -H -b ../../micronix/stand/roms/mon447.bin -B hdcdma \
        -S ../../micronix/sys/unix -L /tmp/boot.log \
        hdcdma0:<dir>/q540root.img hdcdma1:<dir>/q540usr.img \
        djdma0:<root>/dist/mx2.0-8-1.IMD djdma1:<root>/dist/mx2.0-8-2.IMD \
        djdma2:<root>/dist/mx2.0-8-3.IMD djdma3:<root>/dist/mx2.0-8-4.IMD

pass 2 - disks 5-8 (same install5.login, four new diskettes):

    same command, djdma0..3 = mx2.0-8-5.IMD .. mx2.0-8-8.IMD

pass 3 - disk 9 (write install9.login over /.login first):

    ../../src/tools/mnix -f <dir>/q540root.img write install9.login /.login
    same command, djdma0 = mx2.0-8-9.IMD, no djdma1..3

compile - the native build (write compile.login over /.login first):

    ../../src/tools/mnix -f <dir>/q540root.img write compile.login /.login
    ./d1 -H -b ../../micronix/stand/roms/mon447.bin -B hdcdma \
        -S ../../micronix/sys/unix -L /tmp/boot.log \
        hdcdma0:<dir>/q540root.img hdcdma1:<dir>/q540usr.img

<dir> is the directory build_2drive wrote into (default /tmp) and <root> is
the tree root.  The compile marker names a make that returned, not one that
succeeded - check the log for the last "Build successful:" and any error lines.
